{
  "0": "Scale each input item embedding by a fixed training-frequency feature: clamp((count(item)/median_nonzero_count)^(-0.15), 0.5, 2.0). Compute counts solely from each category's training interactions and save the scales as model buffers; padding stays zero and target embeddings remain unchanged. This may reduce domination by frequent history items without adding parameters or changing the GRU, readout, scoring, loss or optimizer. Apply identical scaling in training and the generated predictor.",
  "1": "Replace final-state readout with a causal recency-weighted mean of GRU states, using weights 0.8^(t-j) over real positions j<=t. Apply the same pooling at every supervised training position and at the final real position during inference, excluding padding. This may stabilize interest estimates for the mostly short histories while retaining recent intent. Keep truncation, embeddings, GRU topology, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer fixed; update the predictor writer consistently.",
  "2": "Replace BPR with softplus(neg_score-pos_score) plus 0.2 times [softplus(-pos_score)+softplus(neg_score)], averaged over the existing nonpadding targets. The pairwise term preserves ranking pressure while the pointwise terms constrain score drift and give both observed and sampled items direct supervision. Keep the exact samples, GRU, embeddings, readout, optimizer, scheduler and training limits unchanged. Retain one checkpoint per category.",
  "3": "Reparameterize the shared item embedding table so each nonpadding row has unit L2 norm whenever used for either history input or target lookup; padding remains exactly zero. Retain the existing embedding width, GRU topology and dot-product scorer. Removing item-vector magnitude as a popularity shortcut may improve discrimination through embedding direction. Keep features, history policy, loss and optimizer fixed, and reproduce this parameterization in the predictor writer with matching checkpoint shapes."
}
